{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"regex","title":"Regular Expressions","group":"Natural Language Processing","order":18,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Regular expressions describe text patterns — phone numbers, emails, hyphenated words — and find, extract or replace them in one line. Python's <code>re</code> module.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>search, findall, finditer</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nm = re.search(\"hello\", \"hello world\")\nprint(m.span(), m.start(), m.end(), m.group())    # (0, 5) 0 5 hello\nprint(re.search(\"data\", \"hello world\"))           # None\nprint(re.findall(\"at\", \"The rat sat on the mat\"))                 # ['at', 'at', 'at']\nprint([m.span() for m in re.finditer(\"hello\", \"hello world hello earth\")])   # [(0, 5), (12, 17)]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>re.search(p, s)</code></td><td>The first match (a Match object) or <code>None</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>re.match(p, s)</code></td><td>A match only at the <strong>start</strong> of <code>s</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>re.findall(p, s)</code></td><td>A list of all matching strings (or groups)</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.finditer(p, s)</code></td><td>Match objects one by one — with positions</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.sub(p, repl, s)</code></td><td><code>s</code> with matches replaced</td></tr><tr><td><code>re.split(p, s)</code></td><td><code>s</code> split at matches</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>The building blocks</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>.</code></td><td>Any character except newline</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\d</code> / <code>\\D</code></td><td>A digit / anything but a digit</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\w</code> / <code>\\W</code></td><td>A word character (letter, digit, _) / anything else</td></tr><tr><td><code>\\s</code></td><td>Whitespace</td></tr><tr><td><code>[A-Za-z]</code>, <code>[^!,$@]</code></td><td>One character from a set / <strong>not</strong> in a set</td></tr><tr><td><code>^</code> / <code>$</code></td><td>Start / end of the string</td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code> <code>+</code> <code>?</code> <code>{3}</code> <code>{2,4}</code></td><td>0+, 1+, 0 or 1, exactly 3, 2 to 4 times</td></tr><tr><td><code>(a|b)</code></td><td>Group with alternatives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(\".at\", \"The rat sat on the mat and a cat\"))     # ['rat', 'sat', 'mat', 'cat']\nprint(re.findall(r\"^\\d\", \"4 is divisible by 2\"), re.findall(r\"^\\d\", \"Hi, 4\"))   # ['4'] []\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\d$\", \"4 is divisible by 2 and not by 3\"))   # ['3']\nprint(re.findall(\"[A-Z]\", \"Welcome to GFG 1\"))                   # ['W', 'G', 'F', 'G']\nprint(re.search(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}-\\d{3}\", \"call 834-4324-345\").group())   # 834-4324-345</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Write patterns as <strong>raw strings</strong> — <code>r\"\\d\"</code> — so Python doesn't treat the backslash as an escape. The notebook's <code>'\\d'</code> works but triggers a warning in modern Python.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eraser\"></i>Cleaning text</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.sub(r\"[!,$@]\", \"\", \"Eshant $ is happ@y !,\").strip())    # Eshant  is happy\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\d\", \"\", \"I'm Ashish and 24\").strip())            # I'm Ashish and\nprint(re.findall(r\"\\w+-\\w+-\\w+\", \"Geeks-for-Geeks and works-it-out\"))   # ['Geeks-for-Geeks', 'works-it-out']\nprint(re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", \"too    many   spaces\"))              # too many spaces</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-mail\"></i>Email patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import re\nEMAIL = r\"[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+(\\.[A-Za-z0-9-]+)*\\.[A-Za-z]{2,}\"\ntext = \"Mail eshant@gfg.in or esh@aap.gov.in to contact us\"\nprint([m.group() for m in re.finditer(EMAIL, text)])        # ['eshant@gfg.in', 'esh@aap.gov.in']\n\nGFG_OR_GMAIL = r\"[\\w.]+@(gfg|gmail)\\.(org|net|in|com)\"\nfor mail in [\"eshant@gfg.org\", \"eshant@gmail.com\", \"eshant@yahoo.com\"]:\n    print(mail, bool(re.fullmatch(GFG_OR_GMAIL, mail)))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>['eshant@gfg.in', 'esh@aap.gov.in']\neshant@gfg.org True\neshant@gmail.com True\neshant@yahoo.com False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Correction</div><p>Two slips in the notebook's patterns: <code>[A-Za-z0-2]</code> allows only the digits 0–2 (should be <code>0-9</code>), and an unescaped <code>.</code> matches <strong>any</strong> character — write <code>\\.</code> for a literal dot. Use <code>re.fullmatch</code> to validate a whole string instead of finding a pattern inside it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Without regex: splitting an email</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>mails = [\"eshant@gmail.com\", \"eshant@aap.gov.in\", \"esh@geeksforgeeks.com\"]\nparts = [(m.split(\"@\")[0], *m.split(\"@\")[1].split(\".\", 1)) for m in mails]\nprint(parts)      # [('eshant', 'gmail', 'com'), ('eshant', 'aap', 'gov.in'), ('esh', 'geeksforgeeks', 'com')]</code></pre></figure></section>"}